    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "If $a$ is its own inverse modulo $n$, then $a^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}$.\nThis means that $n$ divides $a^2 - 1$.\nSince $n$ divides $a^2 - 1$, it must also divide $(a - 1)(a + 1)$.\nBut $n$ divides $(a - 1)(a + 1)$ if and only if $n$ divides $a - 1$ or $n$ divides $a + 1$.\nSince $a$ is an integer, $a - 1$ and $a + 1$ are consecutive integers.\nTherefore, $a - 1$ and $a + 1$ are coprime.\nThis means that $n$ must divide either $a - 1$ or $a + 1$, but not both.\nIn other words, $a - 1$ and $a + 1$ are relatively prime to $n$.\nSince $n$ divides $a - 1$ or $a + 1$, it must divide $a - 1 + (a + 1) = 2a$.\nTherefore, $n$ divides $2a$.\nBut $n$ divides $2a$ if and only if $n$ divides $a$.\nTherefore, $n$ divides $a$.\nSince $n$ divides $a$, it must also divide $a^2$.\nTherefore, the remainder when $a^2$ is divided by $n$ is $\\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1",
