    "text": "Since $a$ is its own inverse modulo $n$, we have $a^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}$.\nThis means that $a^2 - 1$ is divisible by $n$.\nWe can factor $a^2 - 1$ as $(a - 1)(a + 1)$.\nSince $a$ is an integer, $a - 1$ and $a + 1$ are also integers.\nTherefore, $n$ must divide $(a - 1)(a + 1)$.\nSince $n$ is a positive integer, this means that $n$ must divide either $a - 1$ or $a + 1$.\nIf $n$ divides $a - 1$, then $a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}$.\nIf $n$ divides $a + 1$, then $a \\equiv -1 \\pmod{n}$.\nIn either case, $a^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}$.\nTherefore, the remainder when $a^2$ is divided by $n$ is $\\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1",
